.TH DS 1 "August 2026" "ds 0.1.5" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ds \- domain search: check domain availability across many TLDs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ds
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.IR NAME .\|.\|.
.br
.B ds \-\-serve
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ds
checks whether domain names are registered. Each name is checked against every
TLD selected with
.BR \-\-tld ,
in parallel.
.PP
A lookup asks RDAP first, using the server list from the IANA bootstrap file
(cached locally for seven days). TLDs with no RDAP service fall back to WHOIS
over port 43, using the server and "available" needle table in
.IR whois.json ,
which is embedded in the binary at compile time. If a bundled WHOIS host is
stale or missing, IANA is asked which server serves that TLD today.
.PP
A lookup that cannot be answered is reported as
.B UNKNOWN
with the reason attached, never guessed as available.
.PP
Some TLDs sell to nobody: a .brand gTLD such as
.I .aws
or
.I .google
is registered only by its own operator, and names such as
.I .arpa
or
.I .test
are reserved by RFC. Every lookup in such a zone truthfully answers "no such
domain", which would read as available and lead nowhere, so
.B ds
reports those as
.B PRIVATE
with the reason attached, and leaves them out of
.BR "\-\-tld all" ,
.B rdap
and
.B popular
unless
.B \-\-private
says otherwise. The classification is the Specification 13 flag in ICANN's
published registry-agreement list, bundled as
.IR private\-tlds.json ;
a TLD absent from that file is not claimed to be registrable either, it is
simply reported as the registry answered.
.PP
The column after the status is what the TLD costs to register for its first
year, in US dollars: the mean over the registrars the bundled
.I pricing.json
lists for it, or
.B \-
for a TLD none of them price. Multi-label suffixes are matched longest-first, so
.I co.uk
gets its own price rather than
.IR uk 's.
These are published list prices, not quotes, and the first year is often far
cheaper than the years after it \(em see the renewal price in
.BR \-\-json .
Promotions, taxes, registry fees and premium names all move the real figure;
supply prices of your own with
.BR \-\-pricing\-file .
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.IR NAME .\|.\|.
Names to check. A name may be a bare label
.RB ( apple ),
a comma separated list
.RB ( apple,orange,bangla ),
or
.BI @ file
to read one name per line (commas and
.B #
comments allowed). Several arguments may be given. A name containing a dot is
treated as a whole domain and checked as given when
.B \-\-tld
is omitted.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Selecting TLDs
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-tld " " \fILIST\fR
Comma separated TLDs, or one of
.BR all " (every registrable TLD),"
.BR popular " (a curated set),"
.B rdap
(only TLDs with an RDAP service), or
.BI @ file
for a list in a file. Without this option a bare label is checked as
.IR NAME .com.
.TP
.BR \-\-level " " \fIany\fR|\fIsecond\fR|\fIthird\fR
Which level the name would sit at.
.B second
keeps plain TLDs
.RI ( apple.com ),
.B third
keeps multi-label suffixes
.RI ( apple.co.uk ).
Filters the
.B \-\-tld
list only; a whole domain typed out by hand is always checked.
.TP
.B \-\-cctld
Only country-code TLDs, the two-letter ones. Shorthand for
.BR "\-\-tld\-len 2" .
.TP
.BR \-\-tld\-len " " \fIN\fR|\fIMIN\-MAX\fR
Keep TLDs of this length:
.BR 2 ", " 2\-3 ", " \-3 " (at most three), " 4\- " (at least four)."
Measured on the last label, so
.I co.uk
counts as two.
.TP
.BR \-\-private " " \fIexclude\fR|\fIinclude\fR|\fIonly\fR
What to do with TLDs the public cannot register in.
.B exclude
drops them,
.B include
checks them and still marks them
.BR PRIVATE ,
.B only
checks nothing else. Left unset they are dropped from
.BR all ", " rdap " and " popular
\(em 368 of the 1658 TLDs in
.B all
\(em but a TLD named in a
.B \-\-tld
list of your own is still checked. Give the option explicitly to filter such a
list as well.
.SS Showing more
.TP
.B \-\-details
Registrar, IANA ID, creation, update and expiry dates, status codes,
nameservers, DNSSEC and abuse contact.
.TP
.B \-\-registry
Which RDAP endpoint and WHOIS server answered.
.TP
.B \-\-whois
Query WHOIS as well and print the raw record.
.TP
.B \-\-dns\-records
Look up A, AAAA, NS, MX, TXT, CNAME and SOA records.
.TP
.B \-\-where
For available domains, print the registry operator and its official registration
page (both from IANA's record for the TLD), any registration restriction the
TLD carries, and the registrars that sell it.
.IP
The registrars are those that publish a price for the TLD in the bundled
.IR pricing.json ,
cheapest first, each with its search page and its own price: a published price
is evidence that the registrar can sell you the name, and a TLD none of them
price says so rather than offering links that lead nowhere.
.IP
A restriction is printed before them, from the bundled
.IR eligibility.json ,
together with the registry page it was taken from \(em a name being free says
nothing about whether the registry would let you have it. That file is
hand-maintained and not exhaustive: a TLD missing from it is not thereby open.
Both tables are bundled, so this part of the output survives
.BR \-\-no\-iana .
.TP
.B \-\-raw
Include the raw RDAP JSON.
.TP
.B \-\-all\-info
Shorthand for
.BR \-\-details " " \-\-registry " " \-\-whois " " \-\-dns\-records " " \-\-where .
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print a JSON array instead of the text report.
.TP
.B \-\-available\-only
Print only available domains. Saved files still cover every result.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Only print the summary.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SS Saving results
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-save
Write
.IR available.txt ,
.I unavailable.txt
and, when they have anything in them,
.I private.txt
and
.IR unknown.txt .
With
.B \-\-json
they are written as
.IR available.json ,
.I unavailable.json
and
.I unknown.json
instead, each holding a JSON array of the full results.
Nothing is written without this.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-out\-dir " " \fIDIR\fR
Directory for those files. Implies
.BR \-\-save .
.TP
.B \-\-append
Append to the files instead of overwriting them. Implies
.BR \-\-save .
.SS Where to look
.TP
.BR \-\-source " " \fIauto\fR|\fIrdap\fR|\fIwhois\fR
.B auto
tries RDAP, then the bundled table, then an IANA referral.
.B rdap
uses RDAP alone.
.B whois
uses the bundled
.I whois.json
alone.
.TP
.BR \-\-rdap\-file " " \fIPATH\fR
Custom RDAP server list, in the RDAP bootstrap format IANA publishes (RFC
9224): each entry maps a list of TLDs to a list of servers, tried in order.
Without this option
.I ./rdap.json
and
.I ~/.config/ds/rdap.json
are used when they exist.
.TP
.BR \-\-rdap\-mode " " \fImerge\fR|\fIonly\fR
.B merge
(default) lets the custom entries win for the TLDs they name and takes the rest
from the IANA bootstrap.
.B only
uses the custom list alone and never downloads the bootstrap.
.TP
.BR \-\-whois\-file " " \fIPATH\fR
Custom WHOIS server table, in the same format as the bundled
.IR whois.json :
a list of entries with the keys extensions, uri and available, where uri is
.I socket://host[:port]
for classic port-43 WHOIS or an http(s) prefix the domain is appended to, and
available is the text that marks a free domain. Without this option
.I ./whois.json
and
.I ~/.config/ds/whois.json
are used when they exist.
.TP
.BR \-\-whois\-mode " " \fImerge\fR|\fIonly\fR
.B merge
(default) lets the custom entries win for the TLDs they name and keeps the rest
of the bundled table.
.B only
uses the custom table alone.
.TP
.BR \-\-pricing\-file " " \fIPATH\fR
Custom price table, in the same format as the bundled
.IR pricing.json :
a TLD mapped to a list of registrar entries, each with a register name and a
prices object holding regular and renew. Where several registrars quote a TLD,
the column shows the mean. Any price may be omitted or null for "not sold".
The register name is what
.B \-\-where
reports as selling the TLD, so an entry without one still counts toward the
column but claims nothing about where to buy the name.
Without this option
.I ./pricing.json
and
.I ~/.config/ds/pricing.json
are used when they exist.
.TP
.BR \-\-pricing\-mode " " \fImerge\fR|\fIonly\fR
.B merge
(default) lets the custom prices win for the TLDs they name and keeps the rest
of the bundled table.
.B only
uses the custom table alone, so TLDs it does not price show
.BR \- .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-iana
Never query whois.iana.org: no referral when a bundled WHOIS host is stale, and
no registry name or URL for
.BR \-\-where ,
which still prints the restrictions and registrars from its bundled tables.
.TP
.B \-\-refresh
Re-download the IANA RDAP bootstrap file.
.SS Pacing
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-concurrency " " \fIN\fR
Parallel lookups. Default 20.
.TP
.BR \-\-per\-host " " \fIN\fR
Parallel lookups against a single registry server. Default 4.
.TP
.BR \-\-timeout " " \fISECS\fR
Per-request timeout. Default 10.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Plain output. Also honoured:
.BR NO_COLOR .
.SH HTTP API
.B "ds \-\-serve"
answers the same checks over HTTP, for a web front end or anything else that
would rather call an API than run a command. It is in every released binary and
package, and does nothing until asked for. Building it out is still possible:
.B \-\-no\-default\-features
drops the
.B serve
cargo feature, and with it
.B \-\-serve
and every server option below.
.PP
.nf
ds \-\-serve
.fi
.PP
.B "GET /v1/check?name=apple&tld=com,net"
returns the JSON array
.B \-\-json
prints.
.B "GET /healthz"
returns a status object without touching a registry. Both
.I name
and
.I tld
may be repeated or comma separated, and
.I tld
takes the same
.B popular
and
.B rdap
keywords
.B \-\-tld
does; with no
.I tld
a dotted name is a whole domain and a bare label means
.BR .com .
A rejected request is a 4xx carrying a JSON
.I error
field.
.PP
The two statuses that are not a plain yes or no mean in the response exactly
what they mean on the command line. An inconclusive lookup is
.BR unknown ,
and a response carrying one is sent
.B "Cache\-Control: no\-store"
because it is a lookup that failed rather than an answer to keep. A name in a
brand or reserved zone is
.BR private ,
with the reason in
.IR note ,
and is as cacheable as a
.BR taken .
Nothing in the server turns either of them into
.BR available .
.PP
There is no
.I private
parameter, and the default rule is the one described above:
.BR tld=all ", " tld=rdap " and " tld=popular
leave the closed zones out, while a TLD the caller names is checked whatever it
is, so
.B tld=aws
is answered
.B private
with the operator attached. That covers what
.B "\-\-private include"
is for, without letting one request spend hundreds of registry lookups on zones
the bundled table already answers.
.PP
A reachable
.B ds
server is an open proxy onto other people's registries \(em every request it
accepts becomes a query made under this host's address. It therefore listens on
the loopback address only unless told otherwise, caps how much one request may
ask for, throttles each client, and paces registries with the one limiter the
whole process shares.
.TP
.B \-\-serve
Answer over HTTP instead of checking a name. Every other option in this
section requires it.
.TP
.BR \-\-host " " \fIADDR\fR
Bind address. Default 127.0.0.1; any other address prints a warning.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-port " " \fIN\fR
Port. Default 8080.
.TP
.BR \-\-max\-lookups " " \fIN\fR
Most domains one request may ask about, names times TLDs. Default 50, which
leaves room for
.B tld=popular
and rules out
.BR tld=all .
.TP
.BR \-\-rate\-limit " " \fIN\fR
Requests per minute per client address. Default 60; 0 turns the limit off.
Behind a reverse proxy every client looks like the proxy, and
.B X\-Forwarded\-For
is not trusted, so put per-user limits in the proxy.
.TP
.BR \-\-cache\-ttl " " \fISECS\fR
How long an answer is reused before the registry is asked again. Default 300;
0 turns the cache off. An
.B unknown
is held for at most thirty seconds, since it is a failed lookup rather than a
fact about the domain.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-concurrency " " \fIN\fR
Lookups in flight across the whole server. Default 20.
.TP
.BR \-\-cors " " \fIORIGIN\fR
Send
.B Access\-Control\-Allow\-Origin
with this value, so a browser page may call the API. Not sent at all by default.
.TP
.BR \-\-details ", " \-\-registry ", " \-\-where
As the options of the same name, and off by default as they are there. Raw
WHOIS and raw RDAP are never served.
.PP
.BR \-\-per\-host ", " \-\-timeout ", " \-\-source " and " \-\-no\-iana
are the options of the same name above, not server copies of them: they mean
the same thing and default the same way with or without
.BR \-\-serve .
The options in this section go the other way and are refused without it, so
.B "ds apple \-\-port 9000"
is an error rather than a port quietly thrown away.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
At least one domain is available.
.TP
.B 1
None are.
.B PRIVATE
results do not count as available.
.TP
.B 2
A startup error, such as an unusable
.B \-\-tld
list, or a port
.B \-\-serve
could not bind.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.cache/ds/rdap\-dns.json
Cached copy of the IANA RDAP bootstrap, refreshed after seven days or on
.BR \-\-refresh .
Honours
.BR XDG_CACHE_HOME .
.TP
.IR ./rdap.json ", " ~/.config/ds/rdap.json
Custom RDAP server lists, used automatically when present. See
.BR \-\-rdap\-file .
.TP
.IR ./whois.json ", " ~/.config/ds/whois.json
Custom WHOIS server tables, used automatically when present. See
.BR \-\-whois\-file .
.TP
.IR ./pricing.json ", " ~/.config/ds/pricing.json
Custom price tables, used automatically when present. See
.BR \-\-pricing\-file .
.TP
.IR available.txt ", " unavailable.txt ", " private.txt ", " unknown.txt
Written in the working directory, or
.BR \-\-out\-dir ,
when
.B \-\-save
is given.
The
.I .json
counterparts are written instead when
.B \-\-json
is also given.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Check one name across a few TLDs:
.PP
.RS
.EX
ds apple \-\-tld com,net,io
.EE
.RE
.PP
Sweep every TLD where the name is registered at the second level, saving the
results:
.PP
.RS
.EX
ds mybrand \-\-tld all \-\-level second \-\-available\-only \-\-save
.EE
.RE
.PP
Several names at once, read from a file, across the country-code TLDs:
.PP
.RS
.EX
ds @names.txt \-\-tld all \-\-cctld \-\-level second
.EE
.RE
.PP
Everything known about one domain:
.PP
.RS
.EX
ds apple.com \-\-all\-info
.EE
.RE
.PP
See which TLDs a sweep skips because nobody can register in them:
.PP
.RS
.EX
ds mybrand \-\-tld all \-\-private only
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Large sweeps get rate-limited.
.B ds
paces itself per host, backs off on HTTP 403 and 429, and stops querying a
server that has refused it six times in a row, retrying after thirty seconds.
Identity Digital runs roughly 250 gTLDs behind one RDAP endpoint with a strict
quota, so a single
.B "\-\-tld all"
sweep will leave some of those unresolved; the summary says how many, and they
are worth re-running later.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR whois (1),
.BR dig (1)
.PP
RFC 9083 (RDAP responses), RFC 9224 (RDAP bootstrap), RFC 3912 (WHOIS).
